---
import Sub from '../layouts/Sub.astro';
import { servicePageSchema } from '../data/site.js';

const description =
  'Foundation waterproofing and damp proofing for new construction in St. George, Washington County and statewide — ICC-approved Deco foundation membrane, scheduled to your build calendar.';

const schema = servicePageSchema({
  path: '/foundation-waterproofing',
  name: 'Foundation waterproofing and damp proofing',
  description,
  crumb: 'Foundation waterproofing',
});
---

<Sub
  title="Foundation Waterproofing, New Construction | St. George UT"
  description={description}
  crumb="Foundation waterproofing"
  schema={schema}
>
  <Fragment slot="h1">Below grade,<br />on your calendar.</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    Foundation waterproofing and damp proofing for builders and general
    contractors — applied between pour and backfill, scheduled to the build
    calendar rather than ours. Based in St. George, working across Washington
    County and available statewide.
  </Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="aside">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>ICC</dt><dd>Approved Deco foundation membrane — applicator-restricted, and we hold the certification</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Yours</dt><dd>The schedule we work to — between pour and backfill</dd></div>
      <div><dt>4</dt><dd>Client types: builders, HOA, commercial, municipal</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">The scope</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Before the backfill<br />goes in.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose rv">
        <p>
          Once the walls are backfilled, whatever is on them is what the house gets
          — the one window to do this right is between the pour and the dirt going
          back. We work inside that window, coordinated with your super, so the
          membrane is down, cured and inspected without holding up the schedule.
        </p>
        <p>
          The materials are Deco's foundation line, including the ICC-approved
          membrane and the clear dampproofing sealer — restricted products that
          require a certified applicator, which is the certification we hold.
          <strong>Damp proofing</strong> handles soil moisture on walls that will
          never see a water table; <strong>waterproofing</strong> is the heavier
          assembly for where hydrostatic pressure is a real possibility. Which one
          a given wall needs is a conversation between your plans, the soil report
          and the inspector — we bid either.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond foundations, the same crew handles HOA, commercial and municipal
          flatwork sealing — <a class="tlink" href="/driveway-sealing">the same
          process</a> as our residential work, at whatever scale the contract runs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec sec-dark">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="rv">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-lt">Working with us</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Built for the<br />build schedule.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="rv">
        <ul class="dotlist">
          <li>Scheduled to the build calendar — we move when the pour moves</li>
          <li>Certified Deco applicator for the restricted foundation products</li>
          <li>Licensed &amp; insured, with documentation on request</li>
          <li>One crew, one point of contact, from first walk to sign-off</li>
          <li>Repeat-builder pricing discussed at the first job, not the tenth</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note" style="border-color: rgba(244,240,233,0.3); color: rgba(244,240,233,0.6);">
          New-build flatwork has its own rule: fresh concrete has to cure and give
          up its moisture before any sealer goes down. Talk to us at the pour and
          the sealing gets scheduled properly instead of squeezed in wrong.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sec-head rv">
        <p class="eyebrow">Builder questions</p>
        <h2 class="display sec-h">Asked on<br />site walks.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq rv">
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Damp proofing or waterproofing — who decides?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Your plans and the code do; we price and apply what is specified. Where
            the spec is open, we will walk the site with you and give you the
            honest read on what the soil and the grade actually call for.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Can you match our pour schedule?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            That is the arrangement. Foundation work is scheduled to your calendar,
            and we hold the slot when the pour date moves — because it always
            moves.
          </p></div>
        </details>
        <details class="qa">
          <summary>Do you take on HOA and municipal flatwork?</summary>
          <div class="qa-b"><p>
            Yes — sealing and joint work for HOA common areas, commercial pads and
            municipal surfaces, bid from a site visit. The scope document says
            exactly what goes down and where.
          </p></div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Sub>
